# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
plato-republic-4.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: plato-republic-4.logic.r2 and plato-republic-4.logic.r2.2 have "loves_wisdom"; plato-republic-4.logic.r2.3 has nothing from it; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows
plato-republic-4.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps plato-republic-4.logic.r2 and plato-republic-4.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "the soul has a love of wisdom" is false. Is S1, "At her love of wisdom.", true then?
plato-republic-4.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 3 ways: plato-republic-4.logic.r2 has "kin_divine" and "follows_superior ∧ disengaged → seen_as_is"; plato-republic-4.logic.r2.2 has "kin_divine"; they differ when only "the soul is near kindred with the immortal and eternal and divine", "the soul wholly follows this superior principle" and "the soul is disengaged from the stones and shells and things of earth which grow around her" are true, which the second allows; plato-republic-4.logic.r2.3 has "kin_divine" and "kin_divine → loves_wisdom" and "follows_superior ∧ disengaged → seen_as_is"
plato-republic-4.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps plato-republic-4.logic.r2 and plato-republic-4.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "the soul is near kindred with the immortal and eternal and divine", "the soul has a love of wisdom", "the soul wholly follows this superior principle" and "the soul is disengaged from the stones and shells and things of earth which grow around her" are true and "you would see her as she is and know what her nature is" is false. Is S2, "Let us see whom she affects, and what society and converse she seeks in virtue of her near kindred with the immortal and eternal and divine; also how different she would become if wholly following this superior principle, and borne by a divine impulse out of the ocean in which she now is, and disengaged from the stones and shells and things of earth and rock which in wild variety spring up around her because she feeds upon earth, and is overgrown by the good things of this life as they are termed: then you would see her as she is, and know whether she have one shape only or many, or what her nature is.", true then?
plato-republic-4.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: plato-republic-4.logic.r2 has nothing from it; plato-republic-4.logic.r2.2 and plato-republic-4.logic.r2.3 have "said_enough"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
plato-republic-4.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered yes, which keeps plato-republic-4.logic.r2, to: Suppose "we have now said enough of her affections and of the forms she takes in this present life" is false. Is S3, "Of her affections and of the forms which she takes in this present life I think that we have now said enough.", true then?
plato-republic-4.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 3 ways: plato-republic-4.logic.r2 has "said_enough"; plato-republic-4.logic.r2.2 has "follows_superior ∧ disengaged → seen_as_is"; they differ when only "the soul wholly follows this superior principle", "the soul is disengaged from the stones and shells and things of earth which grow around her" and "we have now said enough of her affections and of the forms she takes in this present life" are true, which the first allows; plato-republic-4.logic.r2.3 has "loves_wisdom"
plato-republic-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 3 sentences: plato-republic-4.logic.r2 puts it at S3, "Of her affections and of the forms which she takes in this present life I think that we have now said enough."; plato-republic-4.logic.r2.2 and plato-republic-4.logic.r2.3 at S1, "At her love of wisdom." or S2, "Let us see whom she affects, and what society and converse she seeks in virtue of her near kindred with the immortal and eternal and divine; also how different she would become if wholly following this superior principle, and borne by a divine impulse out of the ocean in which she now is, and disengaged from the stones and shells and things of earth and rock which in wild variety spring up around her because she feeds upon earth, and is overgrown by the good things of this life as they are termed: then you would see her as she is, and know whether she have one shape only or many, or what her nature is."
plato-republic-4.logic.r2: argue/answered: M: answered no, which keeps plato-republic-4.logic.r2.2 and plato-republic-4.logic.r2.3, to: Is the text's main conclusion S3, "Of her affections and of the forms which she takes in this present life I think that we have now said enough.", rather than S1, "At her love of wisdom." or S2, "Let us see whom she affects, and what society and converse she seeks in virtue of her near kindred with the immortal and eternal and divine; also how different she would become if wholly following this superior principle, and borne by a divine impulse out of the ocean in which she now is, and disengaged from the stones and shells and things of earth and rock which in wild variety spring up around her because she feeds upon earth, and is overgrown by the good things of this life as they are termed: then you would see her as she is, and know whether she have one shape only or many, or what her nature is."?
